1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:21,160
It was delightful to be in such a place, after long weeks of daily and nightly familiarity with miners’ cabins—with all which this implies of dirt floor, never-made beds, tin plates and cups, bacon and beans and black coffee, and nothing of ornament but war pictures from the Eastern illustrated papers tacked to the log walls.
在漫长的几个星期里，我日日夜夜与矿工的小木屋为伍，对它们熟悉透了——这就意味着泥土地面、从不整理的床铺、锡盘锡杯，咸肉、豆子和黑咖啡，除了原木墙上钉着的、东部画报里的战争图片，再没有别的装饰；此后能置身于这样一个地方，真是令人愉快。

2
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:43,420
That was all hard, cheerless, materialistic desolation, but here was a nest which had aspects to rest the tired eyes and refresh that something in one’s nature which, after long fasting, recognizes, when confronted by the belongings of art, howsoever cheap and modest they may be, that it has unconsciously been famishing and now has found nourishment.
那一切全是冷硬、凄凉、只顾物质需求的荒芜景象；而这里却是一个安乐窝，其中的种种景象能让疲惫的双眼得到休憩，也能让人天性中的某种东西重新振作起来——这种东西在长久的断食之后，一旦面对艺术的物件，无论它们多么廉价、多么朴素，便会意识到自己一直在不知不觉地忍饥挨饿，如今终于找到了滋养。

3
00:00:44,220 --> 00:01:13,870
I could not have believed that a rag carpet could feast me so, and so content me; or that there could be such solace to the soul in wall-paper and framed lithographs, and bright-colored tidies and lamp-mats, and Windsor chairs, and varnished whatnots, with sea-shells and books and china vases on them, and the score of little unclassifiable tricks and touches that a woman’s hand distributes about a home, which one sees without knowing he sees them, yet would miss in a moment if they were taken away.
我原先怎么也不会相信，一块碎布地毯竟能让我这般大饱眼福，这般心满意足；也不会相信，墙纸和装了框的平版印刷画，色彩鲜艳的椅背罩和灯垫，温莎椅，上过清漆、摆着贝壳、书籍和瓷花瓶的陈设架，还有女人的手在家中各处布置的那二十来样难以归类的小花样、小点缀，竟能给心灵带来这样的慰藉——这些小花样、小点缀，人看在眼里却不知道自己看见了，可要是把它们拿走，马上就会觉得少了什么。

4
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:22,950
The delight that was in my heart showed in my face, and the man saw it and was pleased; saw it so plainly that he answered it as if it had been spoken.
我心里的欣喜流露在脸上，那人看在眼里，很是高兴；他看得那样分明，竟像听我亲口说出来似的，答起话来。

5
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:31,930
“All her work,” he said, caressingly; “she did it all herself—every bit,” and he took the room in with a glance which was full of affectionate worship.
“全是她的手艺，”他温存地说，“全是她亲手做的——每一点每一滴都是。”说着，他一眼扫过整个房间，目光里满是深情的崇拜。

6
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:40,420
One of those soft Japanese fabrics with which women drape with careful negligence the upper part of a picture-frame was out of adjustment.
女人们会拿那种柔软的日本织物，看似随意、实则精心地披在画框上部；屋里就有一块这样的织物没有摆正。

7
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:48,820
He noticed it, and rearranged it with cautious pains, stepping back several times to gauge the effect before he got it to suit him.
他注意到了，便小心翼翼、煞费苦心地重新摆弄，好几次往后退开，估量效果，直到把它弄得合了自己的心意。

8
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:55,250
Then he gave it a light finishing pat or two with his hand, and said: “She always does that.
然后，他用手在上面轻轻拍了一两下作为收尾，说道：“她总是这么弄。

9
00:01:56,050 --> 00:02:06,570
You can’t tell just what it lacks, but it does lack something until you’ve done that—you can see it yourself after it’s done, but that is all you know; you can’t find out the law of it.
你说不清它究竟缺了什么，可在你这么弄之前，它确实就是缺点什么——弄好之后，你自己也看得出来，可你也就知道这么多；其中的道理你是弄不清的。

10
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:13,740
It’s like the finishing pats a mother gives the child’s hair after she’s got it combed and brushed, I reckon.
依我看，这就好比当妈的给孩子把头发梳好、刷顺以后，最后再轻轻拍上的那几下。

11
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:21,250
I’ve seen her fix all these things so much that I can do them all just her way, though I don’t know the law of any of them.
她摆弄这些东西我见得太多了，所以样样都能完全照她的法子来弄，虽说哪一样的道理我都不懂。

12
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:23,290
But she knows the law.
可她懂得其中的道理。

13
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:29,150
She knows the why and the how both; but I don’t know the why; I only know the how.”
为什么这么弄、怎么弄，她两样都懂；可我不懂为什么，我只知道怎么弄。”

14
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:58,760
He took me into a bedroom so that I might wash my hands; such a bedroom as I had not seen for years: white counterpane, white pillows, carpeted floor, papered walls, pictures, dressing-table, with mirror and pin-cushion and dainty toilet things; and in the corner a wash-stand, with real china-ware bowl and pitcher, and with soap in a china dish, and on a rack more than a dozen towels—towels too clean and white for one out of practice to use without some vague sense of profanation.
他领我进了一间卧室，好让我洗洗手；这样的卧室我已经多年没见过了：白色的床罩，白色的枕头，铺着地毯的地板，糊着墙纸的墙壁，一幅幅图画，还有梳妆台，台上有镜子、针插和精巧的梳妆用品；墙角里有一个脸盆架，上面摆着货真价实的瓷脸盆和瓷水罐，瓷碟里放着肥皂，架子上搭着十几条毛巾——这些毛巾太干净、太白了，一个久疏此道的人拿来用，难免隐隐觉得有些亵渎。

15
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:07,440
So my face spoke again, and he answered with gratified words: “All her work; she did it all herself—every bit.
于是我的脸又替我说了话，他便满心欣慰地答道：“全是她的手艺；全是她亲手做的——每一点每一滴都是。

16
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:10,980
Nothing here that hasn’t felt the touch of her hand.
这儿没有一样东西没经过她的手。

17
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:14,810
Now you would think—But I mustn’t talk so much.”
你可能会以为——不过我可不能说这么多。”

18
00:03:15,610 --> 00:03:37,600
By this time I was wiping my hands and glancing from detail to detail of the room’s belongings, as one is apt to do when he is in a new place, where everything he sees is a comfort to his eye and his spirit; and I became conscious, in one of those unaccountable ways, you know, that there was something there somewhere that the man wanted me to discover for myself.
这时，我一边擦手，一边把目光从房里陈设的一处细节移向另一处；人到了一个新地方，眼前的一切都能慰藉他的眼睛和心灵，往往就会这样。而且，我还以那种说不清道不明的方式——你知道的——意识到屋里某个地方有样东西，那人想让我自己去发现。

19
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:48,370
I knew it perfectly, and I knew he was trying to help me by furtive indications with his eye, so I tried hard to get on the right track, being eager to gratify him.
这一点我心里十分清楚；我也知道，他正偷偷用眼神暗示我，想帮我一把，于是我竭力要找对方向，一心想让他高兴。

20
00:03:49,170 --> 00:04:01,300
I failed several times, as I could see out of the corner of my eye without being told; but at last I knew I must be looking straight at the thing—knew it from the pleasure issuing in invisible waves from him.
我失败了好几次，这一点不用人说，我用眼角的余光就看得出来；但最后，我知道自己一定正直直地看着那样东西了——这是从他身上一波波无形地涌出的喜悦中知道的。

21
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:06,900
He broke into a happy laugh, and rubbed his hands together, and cried out: “That’s it!
他突然开心地大笑起来，搓着双手，高声叫道：“就是它！

22
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:08,690
You’ve found it.
你找到了。

23
00:04:09,490 --> 00:04:10,530
I knew you would.
我就知道你会找到的。

24
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:12,410
It’s her picture.”
是她的照片。”

25
00:04:13,210 --> 00:04:20,630
I went to the little black-walnut bracket on the farther wall, and did find there what I had not yet noticed—a picture case.
我走到远处那面墙上的黑胡桃木小托架前，果然在那里找到了一样我还没注意到的东西——一个照片匣。

26
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:28,170
It contained the sweetest girlish face, and the most beautiful, as it seemed to me, that I had ever seen.
匣里装着一张少女的面庞，是我平生所见最甜美的，在我看来也是最美丽的。

27
00:04:28,970 --> 00:04:33,080
The man drank the admiration from my face, and was fully satisfied.
那人从我脸上饮下了这份赞叹，心满意足。

28
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:39,820
“Nineteen her last birthday,” he said, as he put the picture back; “and that was the day we were married.
“她上个生日满十九岁，”他一边把照片放回原处，一边说，“就在那一天，我们结了婚。

29
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:43,500
When you see her—ah, just wait till you see her!”
等你见到她——啊，你就等着见她吧！”

30
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:45,170
“Where is she?
“她在哪儿？

31
00:04:45,970 --> 00:04:47,110
When will she be in?”
她什么时候回家？”

32
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:49,050
“Oh, she’s away now.
“哦，她这会儿不在家。

33
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:51,440
She’s gone to see her people.
她回娘家看家里人去了。

34
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,480
They live forty or fifty miles from here.
他们住在离这儿四五十英里的地方。

35
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:56,970
She’s been gone two weeks today.”
到今天她已经走了两个星期了。”

36
00:04:57,770 --> 00:04:59,220
“When do you expect her back?”
“你估计她什么时候回来？”

37
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:01,230
“This is Wednesday.
“今天是星期三。

38
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:07,100
She’ll be back Saturday, in the evening—about nine o’clock, likely.”
她星期六回来，晚上到——多半是九点钟左右。”

39
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,020
I felt a sharp sense of disappointment.
我感到一阵强烈的失望。

40
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:14,840
“I’m sorry, because I’ll be gone then,” I said, regretfully.
“很遗憾，因为到时候我已经走了，”我惋惜地说。

41
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:16,280
“Gone?
“走了？

42
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:18,300
No—why should you go?
不——你为什么要走呢？

43
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:19,890
Don’t go.
别走。

44
00:05:20,690 --> 00:05:21,910
She’ll be disappointed.”
她会失望的。”

45
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:25,760
She would be disappointed—that beautiful creature!
她会失望的——那个美丽的人儿！

46
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,460
If she had said the words herself, they could hardly have blessed me more.
即便这话是她亲口说的，也几乎不可能让我感到更幸福了。

47
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:38,630
I was feeling a deep, strong longing to see her—a longing so supplicating, so insistent, that it made me afraid.
我心里涌动着一股深切而强烈的渴望，想见到她——这渴望那样苦苦哀求，那样执意不休，竟让我害怕起来。

48
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:44,690
I said to myself: “I will go straight away from this place, for my peace of mind’s sake.”
我对自己说：“为了求得内心的安宁，我要马上离开这个地方。”

49
00:05:45,490 --> 00:05:53,040
“You see, she likes to have people come and stop with us—people who know things, and can talk—people like you.
“你瞧，她喜欢有人来我们这儿住住——那些有见识、能聊天的人——像你这样的人。

50
00:05:53,840 --> 00:06:04,420
She delights in it; for she knows—oh, she knows nearly everything herself, and can talk, oh, like a bird—and the books she reads, why, you would be astonished.
她以此为乐；因为她懂——噢，她自己差不多什么都懂，而且能说会道，噢，就像小鸟一样——还有她读的那些书，嘿，你会大吃一惊的。

51
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:10,490
Don’t go; it’s only a little while, you know, and she’ll be so disappointed.”
别走；也就一小段时间，你知道的，而且她会非常失望的。”

52
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:16,750
I heard the words, but hardly noticed them, I was so deep in my thinkings and strugglings.
我听见了这些话，却几乎没有在意，我实在太沉浸于自己的思虑与挣扎之中了。

53
00:06:17,550 --> 00:06:19,860
He left me, but I didn’t know.
他从我身边走开了，我却浑然不知。

54
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:31,580
Presently he was back, with the picture case in his hand, and he held it open before me and said: “There, now, tell her to her face you could have stayed to see her, and you wouldn’t.”
不一会儿，他回来了，手里拿着那个照片匣；他把匣子打开，举到我面前，说：“喏，好了，你当着她的面告诉她，你本来可以留下来见她，可你偏不肯。”
